<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Maitraka dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">475 CE – 776 CE (approx.) · capital Valabhi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kings of Valabhi in Saurashtra, whose capital was a great centre of Buddhist and Jain learning compared by Chinese pilgrims to Nalanda.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The dynasty was founded around 475 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhatarka">Bhatarka</a>, a general of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, who made himself ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> as Gupta power declined. His successors made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valabhi">Valabhi</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>, their capital and gradually assumed full royal titles.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Maitrakas issued well over a hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate land grants that document their administration and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat's</a> villages. They were mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivas</a> but generously endowed Buddhist monasteries; the Chinese pilgrims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a> described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valabhi">Valabhi</a> as a wealthy port and a university rivalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhruvasena-ii">Dhruvasena II</a> married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pushyabhuti-dynasty">Pushyabhuti dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> tradition holds that its scriptures were written down at a council in Valabhi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Maitrakas weakened in the 8th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valabhi">Valabhi</a> was sacked, apparently by an Arab expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, around 776. The dynasty disappeared and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chavda-dynasty">Chavda dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/maitraka-dynasty.jpg" alt="Maitraka dynasty" width="320" height="166"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valabhi_(Saurashtra)_drachms._Late_5th-8th_century_Capped_head_right_in_Ksatrapa_style_Trident;_Brahmi_legend_around.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>475 CE – 776 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Valabhi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhatarka, Dhruvasena II, Shiladitya I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maitrakas of Valabhi, Kingdom of Valabhi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=626&amp;focus=maitraka-dynasty" aria-label="Maitraka dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6610 1370 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Maitraka dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6930 1626L7362 1550M6930 1626L7103 1442"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7362 1550h0M7103 1442h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6930 1626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6930 1626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=626&amp;focus=maitraka-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

